# Comments state invariants, not review history (#595). One list for both
# detectors: the Rust one (source_rules/review_history.rs, apps/gateway/src)
# and the TypeScript one (apps/web/src/lib/review-history.test.ts, apps/web/src).
# Each reads this file, runs its own tokeniser over every comment of the
# production sources, and runs every `refuse` and `allow` line below as a
# planted comment before it trusts itself on the tree.
#
# A comment (adjacent own-line `//` comments are one comment) is lowercased and
# cut into words: runs of ASCII letters, runs of ASCII digits, and `#` (written
# `@` in a pattern). Every other character separates words. Lines:
#
#   pattern <elements>   refuse a comment in which the elements match
#                        consecutive words (anywhere in the comment)
#   raw <text>           refuse a comment that contains <text> (lowercase,
#                        whitespace collapsed), punctuation included; `<n>`
#                        in it stands for one or more digits
#   refuse <comment>     must be refused (a planted violation)
#   allow <comment>      must pass (product vocabulary and stated rules)
#
# Elements, separated by spaces: `a|b|c` one of these words; `<n>` a number;
# `@` the `#` of an issue reference; a trailing `?` makes the element optional;
# `..k` up to k words of any kind; `^a|b` is not a word but a condition on the
# one before the next element: it is none of these (or there is none); `$a|b`
# is the same for the word at that position (none of these, or no word left).
#
# What is refused is the record of who asked for a change and when: numbered
# review artefacts (item, round, finding, rule, issue, `review #89`), a review
# named by its source (coordinator, adversarial, second, a PR or issue number),
# what a reviewer said, and a finding or round of an issue (`#321 round 2`,
# `finding 3`).
#
# What stays allowed, with no list entry: "reviewer", "review campaign",
# "review item" and "review queue" as product vocabulary of access reviews and
# aged-hold review; "reviewed" and "needs review" as the state of data or of a
# pinned list; "preview"; "findings" of the key-safety and history checks (a
# finding is only refused when numbered); "security review" and "in review" as
# a requirement ("a change to this needs a security review"); bare "round
# trip"; "per review" and "after review" (a count or an ordering, not history).

pattern ^access|campaign review item|items|finding|findings|round|rounds|issue|issues|rule|rules|point|points|fix|note|notes @? <n>
pattern ^access|campaign review @ <n>
pattern review finding|findings|feedback|round|rounds|thread
pattern review ..4 round <n>
pattern @ <n> review $queue|queues|dialog|dialogs|step|campaign|campaigns|list|page|screen|flow|incident|incidents
pattern pr <n> review
pattern @ <n> ..1 round <n>
pattern coordinator review|decision
pattern approved spec|issue
pattern adversarial re? review|reviews|finding|findings
pattern second review
pattern re review
pattern full ui review
pattern review of @ <n>
pattern found|raised|noted|flagged|requested|asked|caught|reported|suggested in|during|by|after the? review
pattern reviewer|reviewers s? said|asked|found|noted|flagged|wanted|requested|pointed|raised|caught|suggested|insisted|complained|objected
pattern as requested in the? review
pattern per the review
pattern review|pr|adversarial ..2 finding|findings @? <n>
raw finding <n>:
raw finding <n>)
raw finding #<n>
raw (rule <n>
raw (rules <n>
raw (round <n>
raw #round <n>
pattern review comment|comments
pattern review nit|nits
pattern nit from
pattern round of review
pattern per|after code review
pattern in response to the? review
pattern pr feedback
pattern comments on @ <n>
pattern adversarial pass
pattern pr <n> item|finding|issue <n>
raw (review)

